Stored Procedures – Simple Explanation
=========================================
1. What is a Stored Procedure?
    A stored procedure is a set of SQL commands saved in the database. Once created, you can use it anytime without rewriting 
    the same commands.

2. Why are Stored Procedures Useful?
    Better Performance:
        we use Stored procedures because they are precompiled. This means the database doesn’t need to re-read or re-plan 
        the commands every time you run them. So, they run faster, especially if the same task is done many times.

    Improved Security:S
        Users don’t need direct access to the tables. They can just run the stored procedure, which keeps the data safer.

    Easier Maintenance:
        If a rule changes (like a discount calculation), you only update it in the stored procedure. You don’t have to change
        code in many places.

3. When Would You Use a Stored Procedure? (use cases)
-----------------------------------------------------	
    When you need to do complex tasks in the database (like calculations or business rules).
    Used to execute multiple SQL operations in one call.
    When you want to reduce network traffic by doing multiple steps inside the database instead of sending many queries from the application.
    When you want to keep logic in one place so it’s easier to reuse.



